{"qid":"Q953","names":["Zambia"],"title":"Zambia","endonym":{"native":"Republic of Zambia","lang":"en"},"capsule":"The Republic of Zambia gained independence from Britain on 24 October 1964 as the successor state to the Federation of Rhodesia and Nyasaland and, before that, Northern Rhodesia, a territory administered by the British South Africa Company from 1891 before becoming a Crown Colony in 1924. Kenneth Kaunda's UNIP party won the independence election and Kaunda served as president until 1991, initially under a multiparty constitution then as a one-party state (1972–1990); his tenure saw heavy dependence on copper revenues from the Copperbelt and economic decline as copper prices collapsed in the 1970s. Zambia returned to multiparty democracy in 1991, when Kaunda was defeated by Frederick Chiluba of the Movement for Multi-party Democracy. The country remained one of sub-Saharan Africa's more stable democracies through the early twenty-first century.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Zambia's borders are formally demarcated colonial-era boundaries and are precisely known; no significant territorial disputes affect the mapping of this state.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Britannica: Zambia","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Zambia"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Zambia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Zambia"}],"britannica":"place/Zambia","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Zambia","rulers":[{"qid":"Q105480","name":"Kenneth Kaunda","start":1964,"end":1991,"image":"Kenneth David Kaunda.jpg","imageUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Kenneth%20David%20Kaunda.jpg?width=320","imageL